<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Chalco</h1><p class="st-meta">1250 CE – 1465 CE (approx.) · capital Chalco</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A confederation of Nahua altepetl in the south-eastern Valley of Mexico that resisted the Mexica for decades before its conquest in 1465.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Chalco developed around the eastern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chalco">Lake Chalco</a> and the slopes of the volcanoes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popocatepetl">Popocatépetl</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iztaccihuatl">Iztaccíhuatl</a>. From the 13th century several groups settled the area, and their altepetl, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlalmanalco">Tlalmanalco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amecameca">Amaquemecan</a>, formed a loose confederation. The chronicler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chimalpahin">Chimalpahin</a>, a native of Amaquemecan, recorded its history.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalca">Chalca</a> confederation was governed by the rulers of its constituent altepetl, each with its own dynasty, who acted together in war and diplomacy. Its fertile lands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinampa">chinampas</a> produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> and other crops, and it controlled routes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morelos">Morelos</a> and Puebla. It fought long wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tepanec-empire">Tepanec Empire</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After a war lasting from the 1440s, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-i">Moctezuma I</a> conquered Chalco in 1465 and made it a tributary. Its towns later became Spanish colonial jurisdictions in the State of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mexico">Mexico</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 1250 CE – 1465 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">tribal confederation</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:84.58%;width:4.28%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1250 CE – 1465 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chalco</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mesoamerican religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Nahuatl</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chalco-Amaquemecan, Chalca confederation</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1358&amp;focus=chalco" aria-label="Chalco on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2007 1464 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Chalco lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2327 1720L2322 1714"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2322 1714h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2327 1720h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2327 1720h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chalco. <a href="/atlas?year=1358&amp;focus=chalco">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

